\[
\frac1{2^{n+1}-1}=d. \tag{11}
\]","witness":"Counterexample: take n=1 and initial lengths a_1=0.1, a_2=0.9. The nonempty proper subset sums are 0.1 and 0.9, whose only consecutive gap is 0.8, while d=1/(2^2-1)=1/3. Thus the asserted bound (11) does not follow and is false.","load_bearing":"The construction of disjoint A,B with total-length difference at most d relies entirely on (11). Without such subsets, the subsequent common-refinement strategy does not establish the all-n upper bound."}},"S_valid":1,"wrong_answer_stated":false,"notes":"The stated closed form is correct. The lower-bound tree argument and claiming-stage reduction are valid, but the upper-bound pigeonhole count omits the endpoint subset sums and uses an unjustified denominator."}
